/**

@page page_exahype_performance_optimisation Performance optimisation

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE performance analysis combines the runner's decomposition statistics
with a profile of the numerical kernels. @ref page_peano_performance_optimisation
describes the grid metrics.

# Data access

Solver data resides in rank-local pools indexed by the cell and face slots of
the partition, and every dispatch reads the pools directly. A time step is a fixed
sequence of dispatches, so the traffic per step is the pools it reads and
writes plus the halo buffers.

# Kernel cost

Fluxes, eigenvalues, non-conservative products, sources and Riemann solves
should account for a substantial fraction of a compute-bound run. If they do,
apply the vectorisation and accelerator guidance on @ref page_exahype_gpu.
The CPU and the GPU evaluate the same kernel.

If memory movement dominates, inspect the patch size or the polynomial order,
the number of pools a family holds and the plot frequency before tuning the
kernel itself.

# Rank balance

`Load Balance` describes the distribution of the cells. A geometrically
balanced mesh can remain computationally imbalanced when only a few cells
enter the limiter, carry many particles or execute an expensive material
model. A solver-specific profile or work counter identifies this case.

# Hybrid runs

MPI ranks exchange their face halves while the local cells compute. A rank
with little local work cannot hide this exchange. Compare the rank
balance, the rank-boundary faces and the halo waits before changing the MPI
and thread layout.

Processor affinity can reduce the thread count visible to a rank. Confirm the
runtime thread report and avoid oversubscription when comparing layouts.

# Adaptive runs

Dynamic AMR can change both the solver workload and the rank ownership. The
runner reports the balance before and after each regrid. Profile the AMR
phase separately from the time stepping when the mesh rebuild, the migration
or repeated refinement levels dominate.

*/
